---
import SystemLayout from '../layouts/SystemLayout.astro';
import SectionHeader from '../components/SectionHeader.astro';
import { postmortems } from '../data/postmortems.js';

const paper = {
  title: 'Learning Computer Vision from the Pixels Up',
  subtitle:
    'Field notes on classical CV, and why I worked through all of it before touching a neural network.',
  date: 'july 2026',
  status: 'working draft',
  pages: 6,
  href: '/abir-deol-learning-computer-vision.pdf',
  topics: ['OpenCV', 'convolution', 'canny', 'contours', 'ORB / SIFT', 'homography'],
};

---

<SystemLayout
  title="research · abir deol"
  description="Learning Computer Vision from the Pixels Up, field notes on classical computer vision, by Abir Deol."
>
  <SectionHeader
    variant="research"
    eyebrow="what I'm working through right now"
    title="research"
  />

  <p class="intro">
    not published work. I'm two years into a degree, not a phd. what lives here is
    the writing I do while learning something properly: what a technique actually
    does, what I got wrong, and what it cost me to find out. the post-mortems below
    are the second half of that, one per bug that actually hurt.
  </p>

  <article class="paper">
    <div class="paper-head">
      <p class="eyebrow paper-meta">
        <span class="status-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        {paper.status} · {paper.date} · {paper.pages} pages
      </p>
      <h2>{paper.title}</h2>
      <p class="paper-sub">{paper.subtitle}</p>
    </div>

    <h3 class="sub-heading">abstract</h3>
    <p class="abstract">
      Most people entering computer vision start at a pretrained model and work
      backwards, which means the first time something breaks they have no mental model
      to debug against. I took the opposite route: several weeks working through
      classical CV (convolution, edge detection, segmentation, feature matching, and
      projective geometry) implemented and tuned by hand in OpenCV before any network
      was involved. This document is the writeup of that process. It records what each
      technique actually does, the errors I made and what they taught me, three projects
      that forced the ideas together, and where the work goes from here. The central
      claim is simple: a convolutional network is a stack of learned kernels, and it is
      worth knowing exactly what a kernel does before letting gradient descent choose
      them for you.
    </p>

    <h3 class="sub-heading">covered</h3>
    <div class="topics">
      {paper.topics.map((t) => <span class="chip">{t}</span>)}
    </div>

    <div class="paper-actions">
      <span class="repo-bar" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <a class="paper-link" href={paper.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        read the paper (pdf) →
      </a>
    </div>
  </article>

  <h3 class="sub-heading pm-heading">post-mortems</h3>
  <p class="pm-intro">
    every one of these is a bug that cost me real hours in a repo on this site. written
    up afterwards, in the order I found them: the symptom, how I chased it, what was
    actually wrong, and what I changed about how I write that kind of code.
  </p>

  <ul class="pm-rows row-list">
    {postmortems.map((pm) => (
      <li>
        <span class="eyebrow pm-tag">{pm.project}</span>
        <a href={`/research/${pm.slug}`} class="pm-link">
          <strong>{pm.title}</strong>
          <p class="muted">{pm.blurb}</p>
        </a>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <h3 class="sub-heading next-heading">what's next</h3>
  <ul class="rows row-list">
    <li>
      <div>
        <strong>tensors &amp; preprocessing</strong>
        <p class="muted">
          the same array work, reshaped to (batch, channels, height, width): resizing,
          normalization, augmentation.
        </p>
      </div>
      <span class="eyebrow">in progress</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div>
        <strong>convolutional networks</strong>
        <p class="muted">
          the direct continuation: same sliding window, kernel values learned instead of
          chosen by hand.
        </p>
      </div>
      <span class="eyebrow">next</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div>
        <strong>detection &amp; tracking</strong>
        <p class="muted">
          where the motion detector gets replaced by something that holds identity across
          frames.
        </p>
      </div>
      <span class="eyebrow">queued</span>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <style>
    .intro {
      max-width: 60ch;
      margin: -18px 0 36px;
      color: var(--fg);
      font-size: 1.04rem;
    }

    .pm-heading { margin-top: 4px; }
    .pm-intro {
      max-width: 62ch;
      margin: 0 0 24px;
      color: var(--muted);
      font-size: 0.98rem;
    }
    .pm-rows { margin-bottom: 46px; }
    .pm-rows > li {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 168px 1fr;
      gap: 20px;
    }
    .pm-tag {
      padding-top: 5px;
      color: var(--accent-warm);
      opacity: 0.8;
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
    }
    .pm-link strong {
      font-family: var(--font-display);
      font-weight: 500;
      font-size: 1.05rem;
      transition: color 0.25s var(--ease);
    }
    .pm-link p {
      margin: 6px 0 0;
      font-size: 0.95rem;
      max-width: 62ch;
      line-height: 1.66;
    }
    .pm-link:hover strong,
    .pm-link:focus-visible strong { color: var(--accent-warm); }

    @media (max-width: 620px) {
      .pm-rows > li { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 4px; }
      .pm-tag { padding-top: 0; }
    }

    .paper {
      border: 1px solid var(--line);
      border-left: 2px solid var(--accent-warm);
      border-radius: 0 6px 6px 0;
      padding: 28px 30px 26px;
      background: linear-gradient(to right, var(--warm-soft), transparent 60%);
      margin-bottom: 46px;
    }
    .paper-head { margin-bottom: 26px; }
    .paper-meta {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 9px;
      margin: 0 0 10px;
    }
    .status-dot {
      width: 6px;
      height: 6px;
      border-radius: 50%;
      background: var(--accent-warm);
      box-shadow: 0 0 7px 1px var(--accent-warm);
      animation: pulse 3.2s ease-in-out infinite;
    }
    @keyframes pulse {
      0%, 100% { opacity: 0.55; }
      50% { opacity: 1; }
    }
    .paper h2 {
      font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 2.6vw, 1.6rem);
      margin: 0 0 8px;
      line-height: 1.25;
    }
    .paper-sub {
      color: var(--muted);
      max-width: 58ch;
      margin: 0;
      font-size: 0.98rem;
    }
    .abstract {
      max-width: 66ch;
      line-height: 1.78;
      margin: 0 0 30px;
    }
    .topics {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      gap: 9px;
      margin-bottom: 30px;
    }

    .paper-actions {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 14px;
    }
    .repo-bar {
      flex: none;
      width: 2px;
      height: 1.5em;
      border-radius: 2px;
      background: linear-gradient(to bottom, var(--accent-warm), transparent);
    }
    .paper-link {
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-size: 0.88rem;
      color: var(--fg);
      border-bottom: 1px solid transparent;
      padding-bottom: 2px;
      transition: color 0.25s var(--ease), border-color 0.25s var(--ease);
    }
    .paper-link:hover,
    .paper-link:focus-visible {
      color: var(--accent-warm);
      border-bottom-color: var(--accent-warm);
    }

    .next-heading { margin-bottom: 8px; }
    .rows > li {
      display: flex;
      justify-content: space-between;
      align-items: flex-start;
      gap: 20px;
    }
    .rows .eyebrow {
      flex: none;
      padding-top: 4px;
      color: var(--accent-warm);
      opacity: 0.85;
    }
    .rows p { max-width: 54ch; }

    @media (max-width: 600px) {
      .paper { padding: 22px 20px; }
      .rows > li { flex-direction: column; gap: 8px; }
    }
    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
      .status-dot { animation: none; }
    }
  </style>
</SystemLayout>
